From: Eric Tanner <eric.tanner@harvia.com>
Subject: [PATCH] arm64: dts: vt-sbc-am62l: leave the AP6256 Wi-Fi/BT module unpowered

The product image ships no Wi-Fi or Bluetooth driver (vtlinux-image-kiosk.inc,
KIOSK_WIFI / KIOSK_BLUETOOTH default "0"), but the device tree still forced
the module's power on at every boot:

  vcc_wifi   regulator-always-on + regulator-boot-on on GPIO_WIFI_EN, and
             the pad was muxed with a pull-up, so the load switch was on
             from the moment SPL applied the pinmux
  bt_wake    regulator-always-on + regulator-boot-on on BT_WAKE_L
  sdhci2     status "okay", so the SDIO host probed and power-cycled a
             card nothing would ever drive

Every milliamp of that lands on the 5 V feed during the boot-time current
peak, which on a long cable is what browns the board out. Nothing is
regained once booted either: no driver, no radio.

Drop the always-on/boot-on flags so regulator-fixed drives both enables
low at probe, disable the SDIO host, and pull WL_REG_ON, GPIO_WIFI_EN and
BT_REG_ON down at the pad so the module cannot float on between SPL and
the regulator probe.

Units that need the radio (KIOSK_WIFI = "1") need this patch dropped as
well; see the note next to the knob.

Signed-off-by: Eric Tanner <eric.tanner@harvia.com>
---
--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/ti/vt-sbc-am62l.dtsi
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/ti/vt-sbc-am62l.dtsi
@@ -137,8 +137,6 @@
 		regulator-max-microvolt = <3300000>;
 		enable-active-high;
 		startup-delay-us = <20000>;
-		regulator-always-on;
-		regulator-boot-on;
 		gpio = <&main_gpio0 55 GPIO_ACTIVE_HIGH>;
 		pinctrl-names = "default";
 		pinctrl-0 = <&wifi_pwr_pins_default>;
@@ -150,8 +148,6 @@
 		regulator-min-microvolt = <3300000>;
 		regulator-max-microvolt = <3300000>;
 		enable-active-high;
-		regulator-always-on;
-		regulator-boot-on;
 		gpio = <&main_gpio0 63 GPIO_ACTIVE_HIGH>;
 		pinctrl-names = "default";
 		pinctrl-0 = <&bt_pwr_pins_default>;
@@ -345,7 +341,7 @@
 	#address-cells = <1>;
 	#size-cells = <0>;
 	wifi-detect-power-gpios = <&main_gpio0 12 GPIO_ACTIVE_HIGH>;
-	status = "okay";
+	status = "disabled";
 
 	bcmdhd_wlan@1 {
 		reg = <1>;
@@ -789,15 +785,15 @@
 	wifi_pwr_pins_default: wifi-pwr-default-pins {
 		bootph-all;
 		pinctrl-single,pins = <
-			AM62LX_IOPAD(0x006c, PIN_OUTPUT, 7) /* () OSPI0_CSn1 wl_reg_on*/
-			AM62PX_IOPAD(0x0120, PIN_OUTPUT_PULLUP, 7) /* (AC10) RGMII1_TD0 GPIO_WIFI_EN*/
+			AM62LX_IOPAD(0x006c, PIN_OUTPUT_PULLDOWN, 7) /* () OSPI0_CSn1 wl_reg_on*/
+			AM62PX_IOPAD(0x0120, PIN_OUTPUT_PULLDOWN, 7) /* (AC10) RGMII1_TD0 GPIO_WIFI_EN*/
 		>;
 	};
 	bt_pwr_pins_default: bt-pwr-default-pins {
 		bootph-all;
 		pinctrl-single,pins = <
 			AM62PX_IOPAD(0x0140, PIN_OUTPUT, 7) /* (AA8) RGMII1_RD2 BT_WAKE_L*/
-			AM62PX_IOPAD(0x0144, PIN_OUTPUT, 7) /* (W8) RGMII1_RD3 BT_REG_ON_H*/
+			AM62PX_IOPAD(0x0144, PIN_OUTPUT_PULLDOWN, 7) /* (W8) RGMII1_RD3 BT_REG_ON_H*/
 		>;
 	};
 	wifi_32k_clk: wifi32k-clk-default-pins {
